Each target language runs its own Pipeline whose key suffix is '<translator>:<lang>', so pipeline.py stays an unchanged copy of video-translator's: the shared stages (extract, transcribe, segment) are fingerprinted the same in every pass and skip, while each language's translate stage keeps its own state. A single translate key would let each language overwrite the others' state, so running es,fr twice would re-translate both every time.

The output SRTs are written after the pipeline on every run, not by the stages: output/<name>.<lang>.srt has no translator in its name, so a stage that listed it as an output looked up to date after switching translator and left the other translator's text in place (seen on the real video: google run skipped, nllb text kept). Rewriting from the work JSON is cheap and always matches the selected translator.

Audio is extracted at 16 kHz mono since only Whisper reads it (no Demucs here). The gpt test uses httpx2 because openai 3.x is built on it; plain httpx was only present in video-translator's venv through another package.
